    "id": "two-sum",
    "title": "Two Sum",
    "track": "Arrays",
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "topics": [
      "arrays"
    ],
    "source": "canonical Two Sum concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
    "description": "The input is an array of integers nums and an integer target. Return the indices of two different positions whose values sum to target, as a list [i, j] with i < j. Exactly one such pair exists in every input, and the two positions must be different.",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= len(nums) <= 10000",
      "-1000000000 <= nums[i] <= 1000000000",
      "-1000000000 <= target <= 1000000000",
      "exactly one solution exists"
    ],
    "parameters": [
      "nums",
      "target"
    ],
    "functionName": "two_sum",
    "starterCode": "def two_sum(nums, target):\n    # Return [i, j] with i < j such that nums[i] + nums[j] == target.\n    ...\n",
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": {
          "nums": [
            4,
            7,
            2,
            11
          ],
          "target": 9
        },
        "output": [
          1,
          2
        ],
        "explanation": "nums[1] + nums[2] = 7 + 2 = 9"
      },
      {
        "input": {
          "nums": [
            3,
            8,
            12,
            5,
            1
          ],
          "target": 15
        },
        "output": [
          0,
          2
        ],
        "explanation": "nums[0] + nums[2] = 3 + 12 = 15"
      }
    ],
    "hints": [
      "For each number, what single value would complete the pair for you?",
      "A dictionary (hash map) can remember each number you have already walked past, along with its index.",
      "Scan left to right. At each index i, compute partner = target - nums[i]. If the dictionary holds the partner, return [seen[partner], i]; otherwise record nums[i] -> i before moving on."
    ],
    "solution": "Walk the array once while filling a dictionary that maps each value to its index. At every index i, compute partner = target - nums[i]. If the dictionary already contains partner, its stored index and i are the answer. Otherwise record nums[i] -> i and continue. One pass: O(n) time, O(n) space.",
    "solutionCode": "def two_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        partner = target - value\n        if partner in seen:\n            return [seen[partner], i]\n        seen[value] = i\n    return []\n",
